prompt sha256: b2c5fb6404ab8f2af46f26f9a414c82a95aeac5bef578b877dbd94f6e8a75835

Sitting-record repair for blue-synthesize.
Your last sitting did not attest what it put on the record, so the run cannot yet show your sitting's revision event, stating the synthesis and its claim_count.
Put it on the record NOW — nothing else.
REGISTER AS THE REPAIR OF YOUR LAST SITTING: your register's help names how, and what you then put on the record counts as that sitting's rather than this one's.
If that register is refused, ONE SENTENCE THE TOOL PLACES tells you which case you are in — judge the refusal by that sentence and by nothing else in its wording.
A refusal ENDING WITH THE TOOL'S OWN SENTENCE "There is nothing here for a repair to file: register without repairing, and report what the record supports." means the sitting you would repair owes a repair nothing: do exactly what that sentence says, quote the refusal in the log, and return the attestation the record supports — this is a success, not a failure.
ANY OTHER REFUSAL is a failure to report and not a finding, whether the record does not bear out the repair you claimed or the refusal is about something else entirely: quote it in the log word for word and return sitting_record_appended false. your sitting's revision event, stating the synthesis and its claim_count.
Do NOT re-do your substantive work and do NOT edit the report again; this turn exists only to close the parity gap.
If you genuinely cannot (the duty does not apply, or a tool refuses you), say in the log exactly why, and return sitting_record_appended false with a one-line note.
Return the attestation.
